## Production notes

**Instagram carousel strategy:** The cover slide establishes the hook visually (rainy window or stone arch detail), then each refuge gets its own slide with a tight location descriptor. The first comment serves as both a save prompt and a CTA for user-generated follow-up—this builds community without hard selling.

**Pinterest optimisation:** Tall vertical pin (2:3) with keyword-rich title that ranks for "escape" + "rain" + "London" + "spots." Description is scannable and includes the emotional hook ("heat peaks, crowds close in") to drive click-through.

**Threads + Bluesky structure:** Text-thread format, one refuge per post, natural conversational flow. The final post links back to londonplanned.com and reinforces the waitlist CTA. Bluesky mirrors Threads but compressed slightly for the platform's pace.

**Facebook link share:** Minimal intro, lets the platform's carousel preview do the work (if available). Link resolves to the coming-soon page, which is gated but whitelisted.

**Compliance check:**
- ✅ No "today/this week/tonight" framing
- ✅ No paid product mentions, prices, or tiers
- ✅ No generic "best pubs" SEO bait
- ✅ British Museum/V&A framed as insider utility, not primary picks
- ✅ All links route to londonplanned.com (public gated page)
- ✅ Waitlist CTA positioned as the single call-to-action
- ✅ Educational/useful content (London insider knowledge, evergreen, shareable)